  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/88aeecb46c1f996a7a2a8947a8b5262faa2991992771a960a1e806c2b8915cac.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${M}^{\u2032}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${M}^{\u2032}$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=2$ because $M$ lies $2$ units *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $M$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},-2).$",
